  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$15.05B** of essential oils, fragrances, cosmetics and toiletries in 2025, 2% less than in 2024. Make-up and skin care (3304) were **39%** of it, fragrance and flavor mixtures (3302), mostly flavor bases sold to food and drink makers, were 19.5%, and perfumes (3303) 14%. Canada alone took **25%** and Mexico 8.7%. In January–August 2026 exports were $10.09B, flat on the same months of 2025.\n\nMost large buyers charge little or nothing: Mexico, Australia, Singapore and Korea are duty-free under U.S. trade agreements, and the EU has charged 0% on almost the whole chapter since July 1, 2026. Two buyers charge more than in January 2025. **Canada** has added a **50% surtax** on U.S. perfume, lip, eye and nail make-up and some hair products since September 8, 2026, even when the goods qualify for CUSMA. **China** adds 10% on all U.S. goods on top of its 2019 counter-tariffs of 20–27.5% on most of the chapter. Beyond tariffs, what decides access is the buyer country's cosmetics notification or registration (EU CPNP, China NMPA, Health Canada), covered on the Markets page.",
  "latestYear": 2025,
  "priorYear": 2024,
